```

Revoking `UPDATE`/`DELETE` on `audit_log` from the app's own DB role makes the log tamper-resistant against both bugs and a compromised app — nothing but a superuser/migration role can alter history.

## 2. Get "who" into the transaction

The trigger reads the actor from `current_setting('app.actor_id', true)`, a transaction-local Postgres session variable. `sessionUser` in `auth.js` already resolves `req.user = {id, email, accountId}` on every request, so the missing piece is threading that into `db.write()`. `db.write()` already does exactly one `BEGIN`/`COMMIT` per write, so this is a single edit:

```js
async function write(text, params, actor) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    if (actor) {
      await client.query(
        "SELECT set_config('app.actor_id', $1, true), set_config('app.actor_email', $2, true)",
        [String(actor.id), actor.email]
