Each row in `audit_log` answers who (`actor_id`/`actor_email`), what (`table_name`, `row_id`, `operation`, and the full before/after row as JSON, so you get an exact diff, not just the end state), and when (`changed_at`, defaulted DB-side so it can't be forged by the app). Because it's `FOR EACH ROW`, a future bulk update still gets one audit row per affected row.

